WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:07.757
This represents all the water used in
one year in the Western United States.

00:00:09.745 --> 00:00:15.000
For the past 20-plus years, a megadrought,
made worse by manmade global warming

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:19.161
has left the West drier than it’s been
in over a thousand years.

00:00:19.161 --> 00:00:24.730
That includes the Colorado River system where
reservoirs have been depleted to record lows.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:27.524
Which has people talking about ways
to cut back.

00:00:27.524 --> 00:00:30.631
“Cities are taking aim at the
backyard swimming pool.”

00:00:30.631 --> 00:00:34.893
“Restrictions, such as not washing cars
at home, and no power washing houses.”

00:00:34.893 --> 00:00:39.820
“Businesses and residents must cut their
outdoor watering to just one day a week.”

00:00:40.189 --> 00:00:44.360
But this is all the residential water use
for 115 million people

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.611
across 17 Western states.

00:00:46.611 --> 00:00:51.083
All the lawn watering and tooth brushing
and pools and showers

00:00:51.083 --> 00:00:53.930
use only 6 percent of all water consumption.

00:00:54.768 --> 00:00:58.254
The rest of this tells a totally
different story.

00:01:04.246 --> 00:01:07.800
A team of researchers came up with
these water use estimates.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:12.290
And they found that another 8 percent
goes towards commercial and industrial uses

00:01:12.290 --> 00:01:16.780
like offices, hotel fountains, mines,
and power plants.

00:01:17.703 --> 00:01:19.461
Together with residential use

00:01:19.461 --> 00:01:23.074
that’s 14 percent of all water consumption
in the West.

00:01:23.074 --> 00:01:28.327
All of what’s left in here — the other 
86 percent is for growing crops.

00:01:28.327 --> 00:01:30.621
Irrigated agriculture is the big story.

00:01:30.621 --> 00:01:33.144
All the other water users added together...

00:01:34.034 --> 00:01:36.964
don't come close to irrigated agriculture.

00:01:36.964 --> 00:01:40.350
In the dry western states, 
farms can’t count on rain

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.163
so crops need irrigation.

00:01:43.001 --> 00:01:48.395
So here’s soybeans, nuts, wheat,
fruits &amp; veggies, corn.

00:01:49.162 --> 00:01:52.299
But what’s even more surprising is that
the biggest share of this water

00:01:52.299 --> 00:01:54.666
goes to crops humans don’t eat.

00:01:54.666 --> 00:01:57.285
The biggest water guzzler is alfalfa.

00:01:58.236 --> 00:01:59.810
You might not recognize this plant

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:04.296
but if you’ve ever seen a bale of hay that
looks greenish: that’s alfalfa.

00:02:04.296 --> 00:02:06.920
Unlike a lot of crops, with enough water

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:11.999
farmers in the West can harvest it up to
12 times a year and sell every last bit.

00:02:11.999 --> 00:02:18.019
It's about a 9 to 10 month crop down 
in our part of the desert.

00:02:18.019 --> 00:02:21.620
And so you're farming it 9 or 10 months

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:24.650
and therefore, you're irrigating it
during that time.

00:02:25.232 --> 00:02:29.093
We can group alfalfa’s water with the water
used for other grass hays

00:02:29.093 --> 00:02:30.820
and for corn silage.

00:02:32.722 --> 00:02:37.406
Together, this accounts for 32% of the West’s
entire water footprint.

00:02:40.875 --> 00:02:45.228
And what they have in common is that these
are all crops grown almost exclusively

00:02:45.228 --> 00:02:46.658
to feed cows.

00:02:47.668 --> 00:02:51.132
That’s more than all the residential and
commercial uses combined.

00:02:51.132 --> 00:02:53.760
A third of all the water consumed in the West...

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:55.341
just for feeding cattle.

00:02:56.546 --> 00:03:00.680
Americans eat nearly 4 times more beef
than the global average.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:03.874
Our dairy intake has been increasing
for decades, too.

00:03:03.874 --> 00:03:04.971
And we’re not alone.

00:03:04.971 --> 00:03:09.359
At least 10% of this cattle feed ends up on
container ships crossing the pacific

00:03:09.359 --> 00:03:13.019
to feed cows in Japan, China, and
the Middle East.

00:03:13.019 --> 00:03:21.000
Agriculture in the United States is built
to supply a world export market.

00:03:22.596 --> 00:03:24.260
So what do we do about this?

00:03:25.352 --> 00:03:27.260
It’s clear that letting our lawns die

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:30.271
would make less of an impact than
changing our diets.

00:03:30.271 --> 00:03:32.930
But that solution is a hard sell for consumers

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:34.480
even the most informed ones.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:40.828
In case anybody takes me for like a meat hating
like college professor...

00:03:41.349 --> 00:03:43.143
cheeseburgers are my favorite food.

00:03:43.143 --> 00:03:47.282
And my cheeseburgers come from
Colorado River water.

00:03:47.282 --> 00:03:54.406
So I have thought about it and I think that
before we all stop eating meat

00:03:54.406 --> 00:03:57.127
we should explore a couple of
other solutions.

00:03:57.648 --> 00:04:01.742
Ben and his co-authors highlight an approach
called “rotational fallowing.”

00:04:01.742 --> 00:04:04.900
It enables agencies representing
metropolitan water users

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:06.800
to set a price that they’ll pay farmers

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:10.426
to temporarily stop irrigating 
a portion of their fields.

00:04:10.426 --> 00:04:13.680
Farmers decide whether it’s worth it in
any given year to take that deal.

00:04:14.217 --> 00:04:17.258
And the unused water can then be sent to the
cities...

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:19.322
or left in reservoirs.

00:04:19.322 --> 00:04:24.030
Can fallowing hay and alfalfa 
in the Colorado River Basin

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:28.950
solve the current 2022 year drought crisis?

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:30.150
The answer is yes.

00:04:30.573 --> 00:04:32.139
There’s a long-term agreement like this

00:04:32.139 --> 00:04:35.770
in place between the water district that
serves LA and San Diego

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:39.365
and the Palo Verde Irrigation District
in rural California.

00:04:39.365 --> 00:04:43.121
The deal allows up to 29% of the total farmland
to be fallowed.

00:04:43.121 --> 00:04:47.419
It's been largely well received
in our valley.

00:04:47.419 --> 00:04:49.522
I think farmers like it.

00:04:49.522 --> 00:04:53.560
Bart Fisher’s farm depends entirely on water
from the Colorado River

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:55.821
so he supports the conservation program.

00:04:55.821 --> 00:04:58.181
But he also says it doesn't work for everyone.

00:04:58.181 --> 00:05:00.009
Whenever you do fallowing

00:05:00.009 --> 00:05:04.289
there are economic losers and economic winners.

00:05:04.517 --> 00:05:11.719
And some of our vendors who may sell seed
or fertilizer, they're a loser.

00:05:12.778 --> 00:05:17.509
Jobs, communities, and global supply chains
currently rely on western farmers

00:05:17.509 --> 00:05:21.065
growing vast amounts of cow food
in the middle of the desert.

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:25.979
But as water supplies continue to shrink,
something has to give:

00:05:25.979 --> 00:05:29.842
in how we eat, what we grow, and
where we grow it.

00:05:30.868 --> 00:05:34.807
When you look at the water use on the river

00:05:34.807 --> 00:05:39.529
the short term savings has to come out 
of agriculture.

00:05:39.529 --> 00:05:41.224
There's no...

00:05:41.224 --> 00:05:43.469
there's no other place to get it.

